// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// TAILNET ACCESS POLICY — tail10c441.ts.net
//
// Paste into  https://login.tailscale.com/admin/acls/file  and Save. The editor
// accepts HuJSON, so these comments survive — which is the point: this file is
// the only place the estate's topology is written down as an enforced rule
// rather than as a convention somebody remembers.
//
// ── WHAT THIS REPLACES ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// A fresh tailnet ships with:
//
//     { "acls": [ { "action": "accept", "src": ["*"], "dst": ["*:*"] } ] }
//
// Every node can reach every port on every other node. That is how this tailnet
// runs today, and it means a workstation, a spare VPS and an IIS box all hold a
// standing route to two lenders' live SQL Servers. The SQL relay's write
// allowlist is carefully narrow and completely bypassed by anyone who can open
// a TDS socket directly. THIS FILE IS THE OTHER HALF OF THAT CONTROL.
//
// ── HOW TO ROLL IT OUT WITHOUT LOCKING YOURSELF OUT ─────────────────────────
// Tags are assigned per device and a device with NO tag matches none of the
// grants below — so applying this file before tagging anything cuts every node
// off from every other node. Order matters:
//
//   1. Save this file. Nothing changes yet; no device is tagged.
//   2. Tag the devices (Machines → ⋯ → Edit ACL tags), workstation FIRST so you
//      keep a way in. A tagged device's key never expires, which is what you
//      want on a server and is a second reason to do this.
//   3. Watch Admin → Logs for denied flows for a day before assuming it is done.
//
// Reverting is one Save away — keep the default policy above in a scratch file.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
{
  // ── WHO MAY APPLY A TAG ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  // A tag is an identity, not a label: a device wearing `tag:relay` is granted
  // everything `tag:relay` is granted. Only admins may hand them out.
  "tagOwners": {
    "tag:lender-db": ["autogroup:admin"],
    "tag:relay":     ["autogroup:admin"],
    "tag:bureau":    ["autogroup:admin"],
    "tag:iis":       ["autogroup:admin"],
    "tag:admin-ws":  ["autogroup:admin"],
    "tag:spare":     ["autogroup:admin"],
  },

  // ── THE ESTATE, AS OF 11 SEP 2026 ──────────────────────────────────────────
  // Recorded here because a tag alone does not say which box it is on, and the
  // which-box question in this estate has been guessed wrong more than once.
  //
  //   NODE             ADDRESS           TAG(S)                   ROLE
  //   servicesuite-in  100.115.241.36    tag:relay tag:iis        THE MAIN HOST — SQL relay
  //                                                               + interchange broker. Also
  //                                                               runs IIS and SQL on :4420.
  //   salesmaster      100.106.100.118   tag:bureau tag:iis       Metropol egress 102.214.69.233
  //                                                               (on the 2026-08-27 form).
  //                                                               Runs the CRB relay. DO NOT MOVE.
  //   services         100.72.35.56      tag:lender-db tag:bureau Micromart's ServiceSuite +
  //                                                               SQL :4230. Second permitted
  //                                                               bureau origin.
  //   axemicro         100.103.154.73    tag:lender-db            Axe's ServiceSuite + SQL :4420.
  //   servicesuite     100.124.136.83    tag:relay                The SQL relay in production
  //                                                               today. Drop the tag once the
  //                                                               cutover to servicesuite-in is
  //                                                               verified.
  //   lms              100.92.236.116    tag:bureau               A guest INSIDE the `services`
  //                                                               box. Egresses 102.210.148.110 —
  //                                                               the address on Metropol's form.
  //   vmi3298281       100.125.245.79    tag:spare                Free capacity, Server 2025.
  //   pc               100.101.186.43    tag:admin-ws             The workstation this repo is on.
  //
  // `services` carries TWO tags on purpose. It is a lender database AND, by the
  // founder's standing rule of 11 Sep 2026, one of the boxes Micromart's bureau
  // traffic may leave from. Tags compose; that is what they are for.
  //
  // ── THE TWO HOSTS AT MICROMART'S SITE ARE NOT INTERCHANGEABLE ──────────────
  // `lms` is spun up inside `services`, so they share a site and a /24 — and
  // they present DIFFERENT addresses to the internet. Measured 11 Sep 2026 from
  // `tailscale status --json`, reading each peer's CurAddr:
  //
  //     lms       -> 102.210.148.110:41641    ON Metropol's 2026-08-27 form
  //     services  -> 102.210.148.124:41641    NOT on it
  //
  // So `lms` is the host at that site whose bureau calls Metropol will actually
  // answer. It is not a spare; it is the working second origin.

  "grants": [
    // ── 1 · THE ONLY ROUTE TO A LENDER'S BOOK ────────────────────────────────
    // This is the rule the whole file exists for. TDS to Micromart (:4230) and
    // Axe (:4420) is reachable from the relay hosts and from nothing else.
    //
    // The effect is that the SQL relay's write allowlist becomes the ONLY way
    // into those databases from this tailnet, rather than merely the polite
    // way. A leaked relay secret buys the allowlist; it does not buy a socket.
    {
      "src": ["tag:relay"],
      "dst": ["tag:lender-db"],
      "ip":  ["tcp:4230", "tcp:4420"],
    },

    // ── 2 · ADMINISTRATION ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
    // RDP and the local web ports, from the workstation only. Deliberately not
    // from `tag:spare`: a spare VPS with a standing RDP route to production is
    // a lateral-movement path with no operational job.
    {
      "src": ["tag:admin-ws"],
      "dst": ["tag:relay", "tag:iis", "tag:bureau", "tag:lender-db", "tag:spare"],
      "ip":  ["tcp:3389", "tcp:80", "tcp:443"],
    },

    // ── 3 · BREAK GLASS — READ THIS BEFORE KEEPING IT ────────────────────────
    // A direct TDS route from the workstation to both lenders. It is how live
    // reads and one-off verifications are done from the repo today, and pulling
    // it would break that workflow on the day you apply this file.
    //
    // It is also, precisely, the hole rule 1 closes. Keep it while the relay
    // cutover is in progress; DELETE IT when servicesuite-in is serving and
    // verified. Verification after that goes through the relay, signed and
    // logged, like every other caller's does.
    {
      "src": ["tag:admin-ws"],
      "dst": ["tag:lender-db"],
      "ip":  ["tcp:4230", "tcp:4420"],
    },

    // ── 4 · THE INTERCHANGE NODE READS ITS OWN MEMBER'S BOOK ─────────────────
    // Covered by rule 1: the ingest that tokenises a member's book runs on the
    // relay host and connects over the same TDS ports. No extra grant needed —
    // noted because its absence otherwise looks like an omission.

    // ── 5 · NOTHING ELSE ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    // No lender database may originate a connection to anything. No spare may
    // reach production. Two lenders' servers cannot see each other, which is a
    // promise the Interchange makes to its members and should be true at the
    // packet level and not only in a diagram.
  ],

  // ── WHO MAY PUBLISH TO THE PUBLIC INTERNET ─────────────────────────────────
  // Funnel puts a node on the public internet with a real certificate. Five of
  // the eight nodes have it enabled today, three of them for nothing. This
  // attribute is the tailnet-wide gate: a node without it cannot turn Funnel on
  // at all, whatever is typed on the box.
  //
  // Two nodes need it and they are not interchangeable:
  //   tag:relay   — servicesuite-in, so Vercel can reach the SQL relay.
  //   tag:bureau  — salesmaster, so Vercel can reach the CRB relay. It must
  //                 stay there because Metropol answers its egress address; a
  //                 funnel changes where a relay is REACHED, never where it
  //                 CALLS FROM.
  "nodeAttrs": [
    {
      "target": ["tag:relay", "tag:bureau"],
      "attr":   ["funnel"],
    },
  ],

  // ── TESTS ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  // These run when you press Save and REFUSE the policy if any fails. They are
  // the reason a future edit cannot quietly re-open the lender databases: the
  // denies below are the security properties of this estate, asserted.
  "tests": [
    {
      "src":    "tag:relay",
      "accept": ["tag:lender-db:4230", "tag:lender-db:4420"],
      "deny":   ["tag:lender-db:3389", "tag:lender-db:445"],
    },
    {
      // The spare VPS reaches nothing. If this test ever fails, a grant was
      // widened past its intent.
      "src":  "tag:spare",
      "deny": ["tag:lender-db:4230", "tag:lender-db:4420", "tag:relay:8787"],
    },
    {
      // One lender's server cannot reach the other's. The Interchange's whole
      // claim is that members exchange tokens, never reach into each other.
      "src":  "tag:lender-db",
      "deny": ["tag:lender-db:4230", "tag:lender-db:4420", "tag:relay:8787"],
    },
    {
      "src":    "tag:admin-ws",
      "accept": ["tag:relay:3389", "tag:iis:443"],
    },
  ],
}
